---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import img from '../assets/Presentation.jpg';
import gest from '../assets/gest.png';
---

<Layout title="ISHARA">
  <h1>Blog</h1>
  <p class="subtitle">Gesture-Presenter<br>A touch-free presentation controller built with python modules.</p>

  <ul class="post-list">
    <li>
      <a href="https://github.com/navaminagaraja23-ops/gesture-presenter">
        <span class="post-title">Gesture-Presenter</span>
        <span class="post-date">2026</span>
      </a>
      <p class="post-excerpt"><strong>The Problem</strong><br>Every presenter knows the moment. You’re mid-sentence, building to the point of the slide, and you have to drift back to the laptop or fumble with a clicker whose batteries are probably dying. The flow breaks, and the audience notices.</p>
      <div class="image-container">
        <Image src={img} alt="Presentation" class="curved-image" />
      </div>
      <p class="post-excerpt"><strong>The Idea</strong><br>Presenting should feel like speaking, not operating equipment. A clicker is one more thing to carry, charge, and lose. A laptop keyboard ties you to a desk. Your hands are already part of how you communicate, so we asked a simple question: what if they could also run the deck?</p>
      <p>Gesture Presenter watches your hand through a standard webcam, recognises a small set of deliberate gestures, and translates them into the keyboard commands your slideshow already understands. No proprietary app, and no changes to your slides.<br>Only your device's camera can control your presentation smoothly.</p>
      <div class="image-container">
        <Image src={gest} alt="Hand gesture" class="image" />
      </div>
      <p class = "post-excerpt"><strong>The hardest problem: not doing things by accident</strong><br>Recognising a gesture is the easy half. The harder half is ignoring everything that isn’t a gesture. You talk with your hands, you scratch your nose, you point at a chart. A naive system would flip slides all through your talk.<br>We solved this with two safeguards.<br>
        <strong>1. A lock.</strong> Gestures do nothing until you unlock the system by holding a fist for 1.5 seconds. Lock it again whenever you want to gesture freely at the audience, and nothing will fire.<br>
       <strong>2. Deliberate holds.</strong> Gestures must be held briefly before they register. A hand passing through a shape mid-conversation won’t trigger a command. A hand that means it will.<br>
       The result is a controller you can trust, and trust is what lets you stop thinking about the tool and start thinking about your audience. 
      </p>
    </li>
  </ul>
  <ul class="post-list">
  <li>
  <p class="post-excerpt"><strong>Who it’s for</strong><br>
<strong>Teachers and lecturers </strong>who want to move around the room instead of standing behind a podium.
<br><strong>Conference speakers and founders</strong> who want a clean, clicker-free stage presence.
<br><strong>Demo-heavy presenters</strong> who need their hands free for a live product.
<br><strong>Accessibility-minded teams</strong> exploring alternative input methods.</p>
  </li>
  </ul>

  <p class="empty"><strong>Built by</strong>.<br>Yashash    Yashwanth   Rakshitha    Navami </p>
</Layout>

<style>
  h1 {
    font-size: 34px;
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
    margin-bottom: 8px;
  }

  .subtitle {
    color: var(--text-muted);
    margin-bottom: 40px;
  }

  .post-list {
    list-style: none;
  }

  .post-list li {
    padding: 24px 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
  }

  .post-list a {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    justify-content: space-between;
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .post-title {
    color: var(--text);
    font-size: 18px;
    font-weight: 500;
  }

  .post-title:hover {
    color: var(--link);
  }

  .post-date {
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-size: 13px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    margin-left: 16px;
  }

  .post-excerpt {
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-size: 15px;
    margin-top: 8px;
  }

  .empty {
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-size: 14px;
    margin-top: 32px;
  }

  .image-container {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: center; 
    align-items: center;     
    width: 100%;
    padding: 20px;
  }

  .curved-image {
    border-radius: 16px;            
    border: 3px solid #334155;      
    max-width: 600px;
    height: auto;
    object-fit: cover;
  }

  .image {
    border-radius: 16px;                
    max-width: 100px;
    height: auto;
    object-fit: cover;
  }
</style>
